/*
        unicode_case's table: what a code point becomes in upper and in lower
        case, as glibc 2.44's towupper and towlower answer it (the simple,
        one-to-one mappings; no code point grows). A code point shifted right
        by five picks one of 98 distinct 32-code-point leaves from the 3,915
        bytes of the index, which runs to U+1E95F, past the last cased
        letter (Adlam); each leaf is two bytes a code point, the upper case's
        and then the lower case's, and a byte indexes the palette of the 179
        distances a mapping ever moves a code point (0 for none). So a
        lookup is an index byte, a leaf byte and a palette word, and any code
        point from U+1E960 up, or a surrogate, is its own case.

        10,912 bytes: 720 of palette, 3,920 of index (padded to a quad), and
        6,272 of leaves. test/checks.c's unicode_case_reference_rows holds
        the same answer as 1,362 runs of code points, and CHECK_verify
        compares the routine against them at every code point on all three
        machines; regenerate both from glibc with a program that prints
        `%x %x %x` of c, towupper(c), towlower(c) for every c the two change.
*/
